export const POSTS = [{"id":"N1TrykbLZLeX7UdKhUea","slug":"the-invention-of-the-light-bulb","title":"The Invention of the Light Bulb","excerpt":"What if you could make night as bright as day? The light bulb turned dark rooms into playgrounds. Let’s see how a tiny filament can shine like the sun.","content":"<div class=\"lesson-note\">\n  <div class=\"ln-headline\">\n    <div class=\"ln-kicker\">Invention Facts · Grade 4</div>\n    <h1 class=\"ln-h1\">Bright Idea: <em>Light Bulb</em></h1>\n    <p class=\"ln-lede\">What if you could make night as bright as day? The light bulb turned dark rooms into playgrounds. Let’s see how a tiny filament can shine like the sun.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-byline\">\n    <strong>Prep Portal Science Desk</strong><span class=\"ln-byline-dot\">·</span>\n    <span>Science · History · Invention</span><span class=\"ln-byline-dot\">·</span>\n    <span class=\"ln-level-badge\">Grade 4</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-facts-strip\">\n    <div class=\"ln-facts-label\">Quick Facts</div>\n    <div class=\"ln-facts-grid\">\n      <div class=\"ln-fact-item\"><div class=\"ln-fact-num\">First year</div><div class=\"ln-fact-text\">Thomas Edison filed a patent in 1879 for a long‑lasting bulb.</div></div>\n      <div class=\"ln-fact-item\"><div class=\"ln-fact-num\">Filament material</div><div class=\"ln-fact-text\">Early bulbs used carbon; modern ones use tungsten that glows at 2,700°C.</div></div>\n      <div class=\"ln-fact-item\"><div class=\"ln-fact-num\">Power needed</div><div class=\"ln-fact-text\">A typical household bulb uses about 60 watts, like a small fan.</div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-article-body\">\n    <div class=\"ln-col\">\n      <span class=\"ln-h2\">Meet the Light Bulb</span>\n      <p class=\"ln-p\">The light bulb looks like a tiny glass ball with a metal base. You can see them in homes, schools and even on the streets of Lagos. Inside the glass is a thin wire called a <span class=\"ln-chip blue\">filament</span> that glows when <span class=\"ln-chip blue\">electricity</span> passes through it. The bulb keeps the glow safe from air, so it does not burn out quickly. It is unique because it changes invisible electricity into bright light.</p>\n      <div class=\"ln-pull-quote\"><span class=\"ln-quote-mark\">\"</span><p>The first bulbs lasted only a few minutes, but today they can shine for over 20,000 hours.</p></div>\n      <span class=\"ln-h2\">How it Works</span>\n      <p class=\"ln-p\">When you turn on a switch, electricity travels like water through a pipe. It reaches the filament, a thin metal thread, and meets <span class=\"ln-chip red\">resistance</span> – like a soccer ball hitting a wall. That resistance makes the filament heat up until it glows, just like the coals in a barbecue. The glass envelope stops air from touching the hot filament, so it does not melt. The light you see is the filament’s glow traveling through the bulb’s clear glass. This tiny glow can light up a whole room, similar to how a phone screen lights up when you press a button.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><span class=\"ln-chip green\">Filament</span> - The thin metal wire that becomes hot and shines.</li>\n        <li><span class=\"ln-chip green\">Glass envelope</span> - The clear bulb that protects the filament from air.</li>\n        <li><span class=\"ln-chip green\">Base</span> - The metal part that screws into a socket to receive electricity.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"ln-col\">\n      <span class=\"ln-h2\">Why it Matters</span>\n      <p class=\"ln-p\">Light bulbs let us read, study and play after the sun sets, so schools can stay open later. They help doctors see patients at night in hospitals across Nigeria. By using less electricity than candles, they keep homes safer from fire. The invention also sparked new ideas like street lamps that guide danfo buses and markets at night.</p>\n      <ol class=\"ln-process-list\">\n        <li><span class=\"ln-process-title\">Step 1 - Gather Materials.</span><br>Collect a battery, copper wire, and a small piece of pencil lead.</li>\n        <li><span class=\"ln-process-title\">Step 2 - Build a Simple Circuit.</span><br>Connect the wire to the battery terminals and attach the lead at the end.</li>\n        <li><span class=\"ln-process-title\">Step 3 - Observe the Glow.</span><br>The pencil lead will glow faintly, showing how electricity can make light.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"ln-pull-quote\"><span class=\"ln-quote-mark\">\"</span><p>LED bulbs can last up to 50,000 hours, which is longer than a teacher’s career!</p></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-table-section\">\n    <div class=\"ln-table-title\">Light Bulb vs. Candle</div>\n    <table class=\"ln-table\">\n      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Light Bulb</th><th>Candle</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>Light source</td><td>Electric filament</td><td>Wax flame</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Lifespan</td><td>Up to 20,000 hours</td><td>Few hours</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Safety</td><td>Cool glass, low fire risk</td><td>Open flame, higher fire risk</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Energy used</td><td>About 60 watts</td><td>Burns wax, no electricity</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-features\">\n    <div class=\"ln-features-title\">Imagine If You Invented It</div>\n    <div class=\"ln-features-grid\">\n      <div class=\"ln-feature-card\"><div class=\"ln-feature-num\">Glow</div><div class=\"ln-feature-label\">Create a Night Garden</div><div class=\"ln-feature-desc\">Imagine planting glowing flowers that light up your backyard. You could play hide‑and‑seek without a flashlight.</div></div>\n      <div class=\"ln-feature-card\"><div class=\"ln-feature-num\">Power</div><div class=\"ln-feature-label\">Solar Light Bulb</div><div class=\"ln-feature-desc\">What if your bulb charged from the sun on the roof? It would shine even when the grid is down.</div></div>\n      <div class=\"ln-feature-card\"><div class=\"ln-feature-num\">Shape</div><div class=\"ln-feature-label\">Bulb Shaped Drone</div><div class=\"ln-feature-desc\">Design a tiny drone that looks like a bulb and flies to bring light to remote villages. It could drop a mini‑lamp for a child to read.</div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-activity\">\n    <div>\n      <div class=\"ln-activity-label\">Try This</div>\n      <p class=\"ln-activity-text\"><strong>Make a Simple Bulb.</strong> Use a small piece of copper wire, a battery, and a thin piece of pencil lead. Connect the wire to the battery and touch the lead to see a tiny glow. This safe demo shows how electricity can make something shine.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-table-section\">\n    <div class=\"ln-table-title\">Check Your Understanding</div>\n    <table class=\"ln-table\">\n      <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Think about it</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>Who is credited with the first practical light bulb?</td><td>Thomas Edison</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>What part of the bulb glows to give light?</td><td>The filament</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Why is the glass envelope important?</td><td>It keeps air away from the hot filament</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>How many watts does a typical household bulb use?</td><td>About 60 watts</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ln-table-section\">\n    <div class=\"ln-table-title\">Vocabulary Builder</div>\n    <table class=\"ln-table\">\n      <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td><strong>Filament</strong></td><td>A thin metal wire inside the bulb that gets hot and shines.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Electricity</strong></td><td>Energy that moves through wires to power things.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Resistance</strong></td><td>When a material slows down electricity, turning it into heat.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Tungsten</strong></td><td>A strong metal that stays solid at very high temperatures, used for modern filaments.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Incandescent</strong></td><td>A type of bulb that glows because its filament gets hot.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Circuit</strong></td><td>A closed loop that lets electricity flow from a source back to itself.</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n</div>","subject":"inventions","classLevel":"primary-4","featuredImage":null,"videoLink":"https://youtu.be/XWWgDn0C6DA?si=BAU7h-tU1JrKiKeY","practiceLink":null,"publishedAt":1793949917746,"modelUsed":"Groq (server)"}];
